'''Castejón Baliscano''' (''baliscano castejonenco''), also known as the '''Alpujarran dialect''' (''dialecto alpujarreño''), or simply '''Castejónese''' (''castejonés''), is the [[W:Dialect|dialect]] of the [[Baliscano|Baliscano language]] spoken in the [[Castejón|Castejón Metropolis]] and the [[Concordia|surrounding conurbation]]. Due to the city's status as a major cultural, economic, and political hub, the Castejónese dialect is the most widely understood dialect in the [[Balisca|Baliscan Confederation]], and its influence on other regional dialects is pervasive. The dialect's accent is noted for its unique blend of influences, described as being "similar to [[W:Andalusian Spanish|Andalusian Spanish]] and [[Judaeo-Spanish language|Ladino]]". This linguistic fusion gives the dialect a fluid, musical, and highly expressive quality. The Andalusian influence lends it a soft, aspirate pronunciation of the letter 'j' (similar to the English 'h') and a tendency to elide syllables and drop final consonants. Layered over this is a melodic, "sing-song" intonation from Ladino that retains some of the phonetic features of older Iberian Romance languages. These influences are further shaped by the rhythmic and tonal qualities of the various [[W:African languages|African]] and [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|indigenous languages]] spoken in the metropolis, resulting in a creole that is both musical and concise.

== Lexicon and grammar ==
As a cosmopolitan dialect, Castejón Baliscano's lexicon is a vibrant [[W:Loanword|mosaic of loanwords]] from the hundreds of ethnic and religious groups who have made the metropolis their home. With its grammatical foundation being the simplified, [[W:Mediterranean Lingua Franca|Mediterranean Lingua Franca]] (Sabir) [[W:Contact language|contact language]], its vocabulary has been heavily enriched by words from [[Mozarabic language|Mozarabic]] and [[W:Levantine Arabic|Levantine Arabic]] for terms related to commerce, cuisine, and urban life, along with significant influence from [[W:Castilian language|Castilian]] (Spanish), [[Catalan language|Catalan]], [[Aragonese language|Aragonese]], and [[W:Sicilian language|Sicilian]] due to historical and migratory patterns. Words from [[Kurupiran language|Kurupirangüe]] and other indigenous languages have also been incorporated, mostly reflected in local geography and flora, and a rich layer of slang, or cant, reflects the city's unique counter-cultural movements, nightlife, and arts scene. The dialect's development was also influenced by ''[[vesperiano]]'', the dialect of [[Alessandria]], which served as a major entry point for immigrants and absorbed a wide array of linguistic influences.

